Yes. Material can function as a kind of embedded timestamp.

Not because matter “contains time” in some mystical sense, but because materials are constrained by when they could be made, sourced, processed, transported, and used. Bronze, hand-forged iron, early plastics, Bakelite, aluminum, carbon fiber, certain pigments, concrete formulations, semiconductor substrates—each can locate an object inside a technological and economic window.

So you get:

\text{material} \rightarrow \text{available processes} \rightarrow \text{historical capability} \rightarrow \text{probable time range}

And then aging adds another clock: oxidation, patina, weathering, polymer yellowing, fatigue, sediment deposition, isotope ratios, tool marks, repair layers. Material tells you both when something became possible and sometimes how long it has been exposed to the world.

The deeper move is that material was often a proxy for several hidden variables at once: time, wealth, geography, trade access, manufacturing sophistication, social role, even ideology. A marble statue and a molded-plastic figure can depict the same thing while carrying radically different temporal metadata.

So yes—material used to be a very strong time-coordinate. And increasingly, as materials and fabrication styles get revived, copied, composited, or digitally simulated, that coordinate becomes less reliable unless you combine it with other markers.

Which fits what you were just saying: when one marker stops being trustworthy, look for what still survives the disguise.